    content: `The "MIDDLE AGES" and the "DARK AGES" are not two different time periods — one is the neutral scholarly name for an era, the other is an older, loaded nickname for part (or historically all) of it. THE MIDDLE AGES (the "medieval" period) is the standard term for the roughly 1,000 years of European history between the collapse of the WESTERN ROMAN EMPIRE (conventionally dated 476 CE, when the last western emperor was deposed) and the RENAISSANCE / early modern period (conventionally around 1400–1500, with markers like the fall of Constantinople in 1453 or Columbus in 1492). It is called "middle" because Renaissance humanists saw it as the age in the MIDDLE, between the glory of classical antiquity and their own supposed revival of it. Historians subdivide it into the EARLY Middle Ages (c. 500–1000), the HIGH Middle Ages (c. 1000–1300 — rising population, cathedrals, universities, Gothic architecture, scholasticism, the Crusades), and the LATE Middle Ages (c. 1300–1500 — the Black Death, the Hundred Years' War, the Great Schism). THE DARK AGES is an older, largely PEJORATIVE term. It was coined by the Italian poet-scholar PETRARCH in the 1330s, who regarded all post-Roman culture as a "dark" decline from classical standards; Renaissance and, especially, Enlightenment and Protestant writers then used "Dark Ages" polemically for the whole medieval millennium, portraying it as an era of ignorance, superstition, and church domination. Modern historians have largely DROPPED the term, or, when they use it at all, restrict it narrowly to the EARLY MIDDLE AGES (roughly 500–1000) in WESTERN Europe — the stretch with the fewest surviving written sources, shrinking towns, and a contracted economy after Rome's western administrative system broke down. Even that limited use is criticised, because the very same centuries produced the flowering of the ISLAMIC GOLDEN AGE, the Byzantine Empire at its height, the Carolingian Renaissance under Charlemagne, and Irish and Northumbrian monastic scholarship. So the "Dark Ages" is best understood not as a real, distinct period but as a value judgement about a portion of the Middle Ages.`,
