    content: `FEUDALISM and MANORIALISM were two interlocking systems that organised medieval European society (roughly 9th–15th centuries), but they operated at different social levels and governed different relationships. FEUDALISM (the "feudal system") describes the POLITICAL and MILITARY relationships among the LANDHOLDING ARISTOCRACY. Its basic transaction: a LORD grants a FIEF (Latin feudum) — typically an estate of land, but sometimes an office or a revenue — to a VASSAL (a lesser noble or a knight). In return, the vassal performs an act of HOMAGE and swears an oath of FEALTY, and thereafter owes the lord: MILITARY SERVICE (a set number of mounted knights for a set period, classically about 40 days a year), attendance at the lord's court to give COUNSEL, and financial "AID" on specified occasions (the lord's ransom, the knighting of his eldest son, the marriage of his eldest daughter). The lord in turn owes the vassal protection and justice. This created a chain of personal, contractual bonds running from the king at the top, through tenants-in-chief (great nobles, bishops), down to ordinary knights — a way of raising an army and governing territory in the absence of a strong centralised state or a money economy. MANORIALISM (also "the manorial system" or, in French contexts, "seigneurialism") describes the ECONOMIC and SOCIAL organisation at the BOTTOM of society — the relationship between the LORD OF A MANOR and the PEASANTS who worked his estate. The manor was a largely self-sufficient agricultural unit. Most peasants were SERFS (villeins) — legally unfree and bound to the land (they could not leave without permission and were transferred with the manor if it changed hands). In exchange for a holding of their own (strips of land in the open fields) and the lord's protection and access to the mill, church, and commons, serfs owed: LABOUR SERVICES (working the lord's own land, the "demesne," typically 2–3 days a week plus extra "boon work" at harvest), RENTS in produce, and various FEES and fines (to marry, to inherit, to grind grain at the lord's mill). The manorial court, run by the lord or his steward, regulated village life. IN SHORT: feudalism = lords and knights, land exchanged for loyalty and soldiers (an upper-class military-political arrangement); manorialism = lords and peasants, land and protection exchanged for labour and food (a lower-class economic arrangement). Together they are often loosely lumped as "the feudal system."`,
