    content: `The TREATY OF VERSAILLES and a TREATY OF PARIS are historical PEACE TREATIES — and neither one is the 2015 Paris climate accord. THE TREATY OF VERSAILLES was signed on 28 JUNE 1919 in the Hall of Mirrors at the Palace of Versailles near Paris. It was the principal treaty ending WORLD WAR ONE (1914–1918), concluded between the victorious Allied powers and GERMANY (separate treaties dealt with Austria, Hungary, Bulgaria, and the Ottoman Empire). Its main terms: Germany had to accept, in Article 231 (the "WAR GUILT CLAUSE"), responsibility for causing the war; pay extensive REPARATIONS (fixed in 1921 at 132 billion gold marks); cede territory (Alsace-Lorraine to France, land to the recreated Poland including the "Polish Corridor," etc. — about 13% of its territory and 10% of its population) and ALL its overseas colonies; demilitarise the Rhineland; and reduce its army to 100,000 men with no air force, no submarines, and a tiny navy. The treaty also established the LEAGUE OF NATIONS (in Part I). The perceived harshness and humiliation of Versailles is traditionally cited as a major source of German grievance that Adolf Hitler and the Nazis exploited. A "TREATY OF PARIS" is any of numerous treaties signed in Paris over the centuries. The most famous is the TREATY OF PARIS OF 1783, which ended the AMERICAN REVOLUTIONARY WAR: Great Britain formally recognised the INDEPENDENCE of the thirteen former colonies as the United States of America, and generous boundaries were set (to the Mississippi River in the west). Others include: the Treaty of Paris of 1763 (ended the Seven Years' War / French and Indian War — France ceded Canada and its territory east of the Mississippi to Britain); the Treaty of Paris of 1856 (ended the Crimean War); and the Treaty of Paris of 1898 (ended the Spanish-American War — Spain ceded Puerto Rico, Guam, and the Philippines to the US). THE PARIS AGREEMENT (Paris Climate Accord), by contrast, was adopted in December 2015 at the COP21 UN climate conference. It is a legally binding international treaty on CLIMATE CHANGE in which nearly every country pledged to limit global warming to well below 2 °C (aiming for 1.5 °C) above pre-industrial levels through nationally determined emission-reduction commitments. It has nothing to do with any war.`,
