    content: `The BOLSHEVIKS and the MENSHEVIKS were the two rival factions of the RUSSIAN SOCIAL DEMOCRATIC LABOUR PARTY (RSDLP) — both were MARXIST SOCIALIST groups; the Mensheviks were NOT "capitalists." The split occurred at the party's SECOND CONGRESS in 1903 (held in Brussels and London), over questions of party ORGANISATION and revolutionary STRATEGY. On one procedural vote about membership rules, the faction led by Vladimir LENIN won a temporary majority, so his side took the name BOLSHEVIKS ("majority-ites," from bolshinstvo) and Julius MARTOV's side became the MENSHEVIKS ("minority-ites," from menshinstvo). The labels stuck permanently, even though the Mensheviks were frequently the larger group in the years that followed. THE BOLSHEVIKS (Lenin): favoured a SMALL, tightly DISCIPLINED, CENTRALISED party of committed professional revolutionaries — a "VANGUARD" that would lead the working class rather than simply represent it (democratic centralism). Strategically, Lenin argued (especially after 1905, and fully in the 1917 "April Theses") that backward, peasant, semi-feudal Russia did NOT have to wait through a long capitalist stage — the proletariat, allied with the peasantry, could seize power directly and telescope the bourgeois and socialist revolutions together. The Bolsheviks opposed Russia's involvement in World War I ("turn the imperialist war into a civil war"), seized power in the OCTOBER (November) REVOLUTION of 1917, renamed themselves the Russian Communist Party (1918), and built the one-party Soviet state. THE MENSHEVIKS (Martov, Plekhanov, later Tsereteli): favoured a BROAD, OPEN, MASS party on the model of the German Social Democrats, with looser membership and more internal democracy. Strategically, they held to a more ORTHODOX reading of Marx: Russia must first complete a BOURGEOIS-DEMOCRATIC REVOLUTION and go through a period of capitalist development and parliamentary democracy before a socialist revolution could succeed. This led them to cooperate with liberal parties, to (mostly) support the Provisional Government in 1917, and to condemn the Bolshevik seizure of power as premature and undemocratic. After 1917 the Mensheviks were gradually suppressed, and the party was effectively destroyed by the early 1920s. So the core differences were: elite vanguard party vs broad mass party; and "revolution now" vs "capitalist stage first."`,
