    content: `In WORLD WAR I (1914–1918) the two opposing coalitions were the ENTENTE (Allied Powers) and the CENTRAL POWERS. THE ENTENTE / ALLIED POWERS grew out of the pre-war TRIPLE ENTENTE — a set of understandings (not a formal alliance) linking FRANCE, the RUSSIAN EMPIRE, and the BRITISH EMPIRE, built from the Franco-Russian Alliance (1894), the Entente Cordiale between Britain and France (1904), and the Anglo-Russian Convention (1907). When war broke out, this core was joined by BELGIUM (invaded by Germany), SERBIA (where the war began), JAPAN (1914, seizing German Pacific and Chinese possessions), ITALY (1915 — see below), Portugal, ROMANIA (1916), Greece (1917), and — decisively — the UNITED STATES in April 1917 (the US fought as an "Associated Power," not a formal ally). Britain's dominions (Canada, Australia, New Zealand, South Africa) and colonies (especially India) contributed millions of troops. THE CENTRAL POWERS were built around the pre-war DUAL ALLIANCE / TRIPLE ALLIANCE: GERMANY and AUSTRIA-HUNGARY. They were joined by the OTTOMAN EMPIRE (secretly allied with Germany, entered the war in late 1914) and BULGARIA (1915, wanting Macedonian territory). They are called "CENTRAL" Powers because of GEOGRAPHY: Germany and Austria-Hungary lay in the CENTRE of Europe, physically hemmed in between the Entente powers — France to the west, Russia to the east, Britain controlling the seas, Serbia and later Italy to the south. This central position is exactly why Germany faced the strategic nightmare of a TWO-FRONT WAR (against France and Britain in the west, Russia in the east) that its Schlieffen Plan was designed to solve by knocking out France quickly before Russia could fully mobilise. TWO IMPORTANT SWITCHES: (1) ITALY had been a member of the Triple Alliance with Germany and Austria-Hungary before the war, but it declared NEUTRALITY in 1914 (arguing the alliance was defensive) and then JOINED THE ENTENTE in 1915, secretly promised Austrian territory (Trentino, Trieste) in the Treaty of London. (2) RUSSIA LEFT the war after the Bolshevik Revolution, signing a separate peace with the Central Powers at Brest-Litovsk in March 1918 and ceding huge territories.`,
