    id: 'kb-gap-hist-crusades-jihad',
    title: "The Crusades vs Jihad",
    category: 'History',
    keywords: [
      'what is the difference between the Crusades and a jihad specific series of medieval Christian holy wars 1095 to 1291 called by the Pope to the Holy Land versus a broad Islamic concept of struggle greater and lesser jihad',
      'the CRUSADES were a SPECIFIC series of religious wars roughly 1095 to 1291 launched by WESTERN CHRISTIAN Europe and sanctioned by the POPE the First Crusade was called by Pope Urban II in 1095 the main aim was to capture and hold the HOLY LAND especially JERUSALEM from Muslim rule crusaders were promised remission of sins there were about nine numbered crusades plus crusades against pagans in the Baltic heretics the Albigensian Crusade and Muslims in Spain the Reconquista',
      'JIHAD is a much BROADER and older concept in ISLAM an Arabic word meaning struggle or striving Islamic tradition distinguishes the GREATER jihad the inner spiritual struggle against ones own sinful impulses from the LESSER jihad armed struggle to defend or expand the Muslim community which has its own rules in Islamic law about who may declare it and how it must be conducted',
      'so the Crusades were a bounded historical episode a proper noun the jihad is an ongoing theological and legal category the Muslim response to the Crusades was often framed as jihad notably by Saladin who retook Jerusalem in 1187',
    ],
    content: `The CRUSADES and JIHAD are often set against each other as "Christian holy war vs Muslim holy war," but they are not parallel terms: the Crusades were a specific set of historical military campaigns, while jihad is a broad and enduring religious concept. THE CRUSADES were a SPECIFIC SERIES of religious wars, conventionally dated from 1095 to 1291 (though crusading continued in other theatres for centuries). They were launched by WESTERN (Latin, Catholic) CHRISTENDOM and formally sanctioned by the PAPACY. The FIRST CRUSADE was proclaimed by POPE URBAN II at the Council of Clermont in 1095, partly in response to a Byzantine appeal for help against the Seljuk Turks. The central aim of the "eastern" crusades was to CAPTURE and hold the HOLY LAND — above all JERUSALEM — from Muslim control; participants took a vow, wore a cross, and were promised an INDULGENCE (remission of the temporal penalty for sin). There were roughly nine numbered crusades to the Levant (the First, 1096–99, took Jerusalem and set up the Crusader States; the Third, 1189–92, pitted Richard the Lionheart against Saladin; the Fourth, 1202–04, infamously sacked Christian Constantinople instead). The crusading idea was also applied elsewhere: against the pagan peoples of the Baltic (the Northern Crusades), against Christian heretics in southern France (the Albigensian Crusade), and against Muslim states in Iberia (folded into the centuries-long Reconquista). So "the Crusades" is essentially a proper noun for a bounded chapter of medieval European history. JIHAD is an Arabic word meaning "struggle," "striving," or "exertion" (in the path of God) — a concept, not an event, and central to Islam since the 7th century. Islamic tradition classically distinguishes the "GREATER JIHAD" (jihad al-nafs) — the INNER, spiritual struggle of the believer against their own ego, temptations, and sinful impulses, and the effort to live righteously — from the "LESSER JIHAD" (jihad of the sword) — armed struggle to DEFEND or EXPAND the Muslim community (umma). Classical Islamic jurisprudence developed detailed rules on jihad of the sword: who has the authority to declare it, the requirement to first offer terms, the prohibition on killing non-combatants, women, children, and the elderly, and the protection of places of worship. Because jihad is a living legal-theological category, the Muslim world's military response to the Crusaders was frequently framed as jihad — most famously by SALADIN, who unified Egypt and Syria and recaptured Jerusalem in 1187.`,
